//! The base look's hue table: a raw's display-referred colour moved by hue, chroma and lightness
//! (OkLCh), after the tone map and before the output conversion. The [`finish`](crate::finish)
//! stage applies it to raws (the Color profile's look, the base of the others); the GPU path reads
//! the same table ([`TABLE`]) and interpolates it the same way.
//!
//! Nodes are 24 hues (15° apart, from OkLab's +a axis) × 5 chromas (0, 0.05 … 0.20; beyond the
//! last, its value) × 6 lightnesses (0, 0.2 … 1.0). Each holds a hue shift (radians) and the
//! natural logs of a chroma and a lightness scale; between nodes the values are trilinear (hue
//! wrapping round). Chroma-0 nodes are zero, so neutral stays neutral.
//!
//! The values were fitted black-box to Lightroom exports (Adobe Color) of three raws (warm greys
//! and a gold-lettered die; a room with blues, greens, reds, wood and pink; a cyan figure on wood)
//! and Lightcraft's renders of them at the same settings, each photo's white-balance difference
//! fitted out first, by regularised least squares that keeps hues the photo lacks at no change,
//! smooths across neighbours and balances rare colours against common ones (the saturated gold of a
//! die against the grey resin around it) and weighs the photos equally. No number comes from
//! Adobe's software.

use lightcraft_color::perceptual::{lab_to_lch, lch_to_lab, oklab_from_2020, oklab_to_2020};

/// Hue, chroma and lightness divisions.
pub const NH: usize = 24;
pub const NC: usize = 5;
pub const NL: usize = 6;
/// Chroma and lightness between nodes.
pub const C_STEP: f32 = 0.05;
pub const L_STEP: f32 = 0.2;

/// OkLab chroma at or below which a colour is neutral and left alone: a grey's computed chroma is
/// float noise (up to ~1e-4), which would otherwise pick up a sliver of the next node's values.
pub const NEUTRAL_CHROMA: f32 = 5e-4;

